# ---------------- Purchasing ----------------
{
 "slug":"what-is-a-blanket-purchase-order","tag":"Purchasing",
 "c1":"#6d28d9","c2":"#a78bfa","oglabel":"Purchasing",
 "ogline1":"What Is a Blanket","ogline2":"Purchase Order","ogtag":"free purchase orders, no sign-up",
 "subject":"a warehouse office with a clipboard and recurring order documents",
 "title":"What Is a Blanket Purchase Order?",
 "ogtitle":"What Is a Blanket Purchase Order?",
 "cardh2":"Blanket Purchase Orders",
 "carddesc":"A blanket PO covers repeat purchases over time under one agreement. Here is what it is and when to use one.",
 "desc":"What a blanket purchase order is: a single PO covering multiple deliveries over a period at agreed prices, how it differs from a standard PO, its benefits, and what to watch for. Clear, practical guidance.",
 "lead":"A <b>blanket purchase order</b> is one PO that covers <b>repeat purchases over time</b> instead of a single order. For things you buy again and again, it saves a stack of paperwork. Here is how it works.",
 "sections":[
  {"h2":"What it is","html":"<p>A blanket PO (sometimes a standing order) sets out an agreement to buy <b>goods or services repeatedly over a period</b> &mdash; at agreed prices, up to a total value or quantity &mdash; under a single order. You then draw against it with <b>releases</b> as you need stock, rather than raising a fresh PO each time.</p>"},
  {"h2":"Blanket vs standard PO","html":"<p>A <b>standard PO</b> is one order for one delivery. A <b>blanket PO</b> is one order for many deliveries over time. The blanket version locks in pricing and terms once, then lets routine reorders happen quickly without renegotiating or re-approving each one.</p>"},
  {"h2":"Why use one","html":"<ul><li><b>Less paperwork</b> &mdash; one PO instead of dozens</li><li><b>Locked-in pricing</b> for the period</li><li>Faster reordering through releases</li><li>Easier budgeting against a known total</li></ul>"},
  {"h2":"What to watch","html":"<p>Set a clear <b>expiry and a cap</b> (total value or quantity) so a blanket PO doesn&rsquo;t run open-ended, and track releases against it so you know how much is left. Review pricing at renewal &mdash; a rate locked a year ago may no longer be competitive.</p>"},
 ],
 "faq":[
  {"q":"What is a blanket purchase order?","a":"A single purchase order covering repeated purchases over a period at agreed prices, up to a set total. You draw against it with releases instead of raising a new PO for each order."},
  {"q":"How is a blanket PO different from a standard PO?","a":"A standard PO is one order for one delivery. A blanket PO is one order covering many deliveries over time, locking in pricing and terms so routine reorders are quick."},
  {"q":"When should I use a blanket purchase order?","a":"For goods or services you buy repeatedly from the same supplier. It cuts paperwork, locks in pricing for the period, and speeds up reordering. Set a cap and expiry so it doesn't run open-ended."},
 ],
 "related":[{"href":"/en/purchase-order","label":"Create a purchase order"},{"href":"/en/blog/what-is-a-purchase-order","label":"What is a purchase order?"},{"href":"/en/blog/how-to-write-a-purchase-order","label":"How to write a purchase order"}],
 "cta_href":"/en/purchase-order","cta_label":"Create a purchase order free",
},
{
 "slug":"three-way-matching-explained","tag":"Purchasing",
 "c1":"#6d28d9","c2":"#a78bfa","oglabel":"Purchasing",
 "ogline1":"Three-Way Matching","ogline2":"Explained","ogtag":"free documents, no sign-up",
 "subject":"three documents being compared on a desk with a pen",
 "title":"Three-Way Matching Explained (PO, GRN, Invoice)",
 "ogtitle":"Three-Way Matching Explained",
 "cardh2":"Three-Way Matching",
 "carddesc":"Three-way matching checks the PO, goods received note and invoice agree before you pay. Here is how it works.",
 "desc":"Three-way matching explained: comparing the purchase order, goods received note and invoice before paying, what each document confirms, why it prevents overpaying, and how small businesses can apply it.",
 "lead":"<b>Three-way matching</b> is a simple control that stops you paying for the wrong thing: you check the <b>purchase order, the goods received note, and the invoice</b> all agree before releasing payment. Here is how it works.",
 "sections":[
  {"h2":"The three documents","html":"<ul><li><b>Purchase order</b> &mdash; what you ordered, at what price</li><li><b>Goods received note (GRN)</b> &mdash; what actually arrived</li><li><b>Invoice</b> &mdash; what the supplier is billing you</li></ul><p>Each is created by a different step, so comparing them reveals any mismatch.</p>"},
  {"h2":"How the match works","html":"<p>Before paying, you confirm the three line up: the invoice <b>quantities and prices</b> match the PO, and the quantities match what the GRN says was received. If the invoice bills 100 units but 90 arrived, or the price is higher than ordered, the match <b>fails</b> and the invoice is queried before any money moves.</p>"},
  {"h2":"Why it matters","html":"<p>Three-way matching catches <b>overbilling, short deliveries, duplicate invoices and price creep</b> before you pay, not after. It turns &ldquo;I think that&rsquo;s right&rdquo; into a documented check, which is why it&rsquo;s a standard control in accounts payable.</p>"},
  {"h2":"For a small business","html":"<p>You don&rsquo;t need software to do it. For any order that matters, keep the <b>PO, note what arrived, and compare both to the invoice</b> before paying. Even this light version catches the costly mistakes &mdash; paying for goods that never came, or a price that quietly jumped.</p>"},
 ],
 "faq":[
  {"q":"What is three-way matching?","a":"A check that compares the purchase order, the goods received note and the invoice before payment. If the quantities and prices across all three agree, the invoice is approved; if not, it's queried first."},
  {"q":"Why is three-way matching important?","a":"It catches overbilling, short deliveries, duplicate invoices and price increases before you pay, rather than after. It's a standard accounts-payable control against paying for the wrong thing."},
  {"q":"Can a small business do three-way matching?","a":"Yes, without software. Keep the purchase order, record what actually arrived, and compare both against the invoice before paying. Even this simple version prevents the costly errors."},
 ],
 "related":[{"href":"/en/purchase-order","label":"Create a purchase order"},{"href":"/en/blog/what-is-a-goods-received-note","label":"What is a goods received note?"},{"href":"/en/blog/purchase-order-vs-invoice","label":"Purchase order vs invoice"}],
 "cta_href":"/en/purchase-order","cta_label":"Create a purchase order free",
},

