  {"h2":"Match the invoice to the work","html":"<ul><li><b>Live gigs</b> &mdash; invoice a performance fee, often with a deposit to hold the date</li><li><b>Session work</b> &mdash; bill per hour, per song, or a day rate</li><li><b>Teaching</b> &mdash; invoice per lesson or a block of lessons</li><li><b>Licensing / recording</b> &mdash; bill the agreed fee and state the usage granted</li></ul>"},
  {"h2":"Take a deposit for bookings","html":"<p>For gigs and events, a <b>deposit secures the date</b> and protects you against last-minute cancellations. Note the deposit as paid on the final invoice and bill the balance on or before the performance day. Put your <b>cancellation terms</b> in writing.</p>"},
  {"h2":"Expenses and travel","html":"<p>If the booking involves <b>travel, equipment hire or extra musicians</b>, list those separately from your fee so the client sees what&rsquo;s performance and what&rsquo;s cost. Agree in advance who covers travel for out-of-town gigs.</p>"},
  {"h2":"Keep clean records","html":"<p>Income from many small sources adds up fast, so <b>number every invoice and keep copies</b>. Good records make your tax return simple and give you a clear picture of which work &mdash; gigs, sessions or teaching &mdash; actually pays.</p>"},
